## Summary

Builtin `memory_search` repeatedly exceeds its 30-second deadline on an index with 18,340 vectors (1536 dimensions). Stage profiling isolates most of the delay to SQLite/vec0 retrieval, not the embedding request. The vec0 allocation is sparse: 53 blocks / 54,272 allocated slots for 18,340 active IDs. Rebuilding only the derived vec0 table into 18 blocks, with every active ID and Float32 vector byte preserved, substantially improves cold-cache retrieval on the same frozen corpus.

This report concerns the **read-side cost of sparse vector allocation**. It is not a new reproduction of the old main-thread publication stall in #134575; that publication work already runs through the worker in this release. Related: #158917 (timeout symptom), #174971 (read-only child cache improvement).

## Environment and scope

- OpenClaw 2026.10.1; Linux x86_64, Node 24.18.0; 2 vCPU / 8 GiB RAM.
- Builtin memory-core, sqlite-vec v0.1.9, OpenAI embeddings, 1536 dimensions; both memory and sessions indexed.
- Production Gateway stays running with no restart during the observed timeout. A restart had not made the problem stay resolved.
- No claim of reproducing on current `main`, whose vector backend architecture has changed.
- Disclosure: the primary measurements use local KNN optimizations (64 MiB child cache and source-embedding Float32 projection to avoid repeated vec0 point-vector reads). A separate exact stock-2026.10.1 compiled KNN query-body control with its original 2 MiB cache also failed to finish within a bounded 35-second diagnostic run on the frozen sparse fixture. That control is a component harness, **not a complete unmodified Gateway reproduction**.

## Measurements

Read-only native manager, unchanged live index, ordinary host load:

| Stage | Duration |
|---|---:|
| Keyword preparation | 2,504 ms |
| Keyword fallback | 2,868 ms |
| Embedding request | 1,309 ms |
| Vector stage before overall deadline | 23,278 ms |
| Overall search | 30,025 ms, aborted; lexical partial results only |

An independent stored-vector KNN probe (no embedding API request), using the native 200-candidate retrieval with oversample 8:

- 47,432 ms wall time, approximately 2.63 CPU seconds.
- 458,485,760 physical read bytes in that process.
- Float32 projection callback accounted for only 161 ms over 1,600 candidates.
- Query plan uses vec0 followed by an indexed chunk-ID lookup, not an ordinary full `memory_index_chunks` scan.

### Frozen-fixture compaction experiment

A consistent private snapshot was taken first; no live index, message, or source record was replaced for this experiment. Only its derived vec0 table was rebuilt, in an atomic transaction, from existing source embeddings. All 18,340 IDs and actual Float32 vector bytes were checked before commit.

| | Original | Compacted |
|---|---:|---:|
| Active vector IDs | 18,340 | 18,340 |
| Allocated blocks (1024 slots each) | 53 | 18 |
| Vector blob bytes | 333,447,168 | 113,246,208 |

Four query/source combinations returned exactly the same 200 rows, order, and distances across both fixtures (memory-only, sessions-only, both sources; older and newer query vectors).

Controlled cold-cache ABBA runs using `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` **only on the clean private fixture files**, never global cache dropping or production files:

1. Original: 47,243 ms.
2. Compacted: 18,963 ms.
3. Compacted: 19,000 ms.
4. Original: 46,195 ms.

All four returned byte-identical serialized 200-row results. This is roughly 2.45x for these controlled cold reads, not a universal performance claim. An earlier much larger apparent warm/cold speedup was discarded because cache warmth confounded it.

The full native manager pipeline against the isolated compacted fixture completed in 11,657 ms, with five semantic hits (embedding 2,238 ms, vector 5,070 ms). This is an isolated validation, **not evidence of a permanent live fix**. Host memory/CPU contention may contribute separately; this report does not claim compaction cures a Gateway memory leak.

## Mechanism to investigate

In sqlite-vec v0.1.9, KNN reads each allocated vector chunk blob before applying validity bits. Deletes can therefore leave a read working set much larger than the active-vector count suggests. Inserts seek slots in the latest chunk, while a chunk is removed on deletion only when entirely empty.

OpenClaw's source replacement clears all previous vectors for a changed source and reinserts the incoming chunks, including otherwise unchanged chunks. That is a plausible ongoing source of sparse allocation; the production history was not traced event-by-event, so it is not proof that every sparse block came from that specific path.

Relevant versioned sources:

- [OpenClaw 2026.10.1 source-index kernel](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/v2026.10.1/extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-source-index-kernel.ts)
- [OpenClaw 2026.10.1 KNN retrieval](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/v2026.10.1/extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-search-knn.ts)
- [sqlite-vec v0.1.9 source](https://github.com/asg017/sqlite-vec/blob/v0.1.9/sqlite-vec.c)

## Expected behavior / requested investigation

Bounded semantic recall should not degrade indefinitely as a derived vector index becomes sparse. Please consider:

1. Exposing active versus allocated vector slots/bytes in diagnostics.
2. An explicit, ownership-aware, atomic derived-vector compaction path with generation leases, cancellation, rollback, and complete ID/vector parity checks, without replacing the whole agent database.
3. Avoiding vector delete/reinsert churn for provably unchanged chunks while preserving provenance, source-forget semantics, rebuild markers, and bounded publication staging.
4. Testing sparse, repeatedly updated indexes under cold-cache/load, not only a freshly built index. Increasing timeout or restarting alone does not address this working-set amplification.

## Public synthetic component reproduction

The script below uses **only generated vectors**, no private corpus, account configuration, or API access. It demonstrates the allocation/read-working-set amplification and verifies equal top-20 results. It is intentionally small and does **not** assert a 30-second timeout on every machine, nor does it exercise the entire OpenClaw indexing lifecycle.

Run with Node's `node:sqlite` and an installed sqlite-vec v0.1.9 loadable extension:

`node repro.mjs /absolute/path/to/vec0.so`

Observed output: sparse has 2,048 active IDs in 6 blocks / 37,748,736 vector bytes; packed has the same 2,048 IDs in 2 blocks / 12,582,912 bytes; exact top-20 parity passes.

```javascript
// Component reproduction only: no OpenClaw config, private corpus, or API calls.
// Usage: node public-vec0-fragmentation.mjs /absolute/path/to/vec0.so
import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';
import { mkdtempSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
const extension = process.argv[2];
if (!extension) throw Error('Supply the installed sqlite-vec loadable extension path');
const directory = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'vec0-fragmentation-'));
const db = new DatabaseSync(join(directory, 'synthetic.sqlite'), { allowExtension: true });
try {
  db.loadExtension(extension); db.enableLoadExtension(false);
  db.exec(`CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE sparse USING vec0(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, embedding FLOAT[1536]);
    CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE packed USING vec0(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, embedding FLOAT[1536]);
    BEGIN IMMEDIATE`);
  const vector = i => { const a = new Float32Array(1536); for (let j=0;j<a.length;j++) a[j]=Math.sin((i+1)*(j+1)*0.001); return Buffer.from(a.buffer); };
  const addSparse = db.prepare('INSERT INTO sparse(id,embedding) VALUES (?,?)');
  const addPacked = db.prepare('INSERT INTO packed(id,embedding) VALUES (?,?)');
  const remove = db.prepare('DELETE FROM sparse WHERE id=?');
  for(let i=0;i<6144;i++) addSparse.run(String(i),vector(i));
  for(let i=0;i<6144;i++) { if(i%3) remove.run(String(i)); else addPacked.run(String(i),vector(i)); }
  db.exec('COMMIT');
  const stats = table => ({ active: db.prepare(`SELECT count(*) n FROM ${table}_rowids`).get().n,
    blocks: db.prepare(`SELECT count(*) n FROM ${table}_chunks`).get().n,
    vectorBytes: db.prepare(`SELECT sum(length(vectors)) n FROM ${table}_vector_chunks00`).get().n });
  const query = table => db.prepare(`SELECT id,distance FROM ${table} WHERE embedding MATCH ? AND k=20 ORDER BY distance`).all(vector(3000));
  assert.deepEqual(query('sparse'),query('packed'));
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ sqliteVec: db.prepare('SELECT vec_version() v').get().v,
    dimensions:1536, sparse:stats('sparse'), packed:stats('packed'), exactTop20Parity:true }, null, 2));
} finally { db.close(); rmSync(directory, {recursive:true,force:true}); }
```

### Summary

`openclaw doctor --non-interactive` with no `--fix`, `--repair` or `--yes` downloads and installs plugin packages, and so does a doctor run without a terminal. The docs and CLI help say non-interactive mode applies only safe migrations. For us, the package was the external Telnyx *inference* provider. Doctor picked it only because `TELNYX_API_KEY`, which the bundled Voice Call plugin uses for its Telnyx carrier, was in the environment.

### Versions

- **Incident:** 2026.10.5, installed globally with npm on macOS arm64, Node 26, Gateway running under a launchd LaunchAgent.
- **Still reproduces:** 2026.10.8 (`dc2523e`) and main (`5958c81`). The patch below also applies cleanly to main at `024e7b34f`.

### What happened

A weekly unattended job ran `openclaw doctor --non-interactive` on 2026.10.5.

- Confirmed (observed): it installed `@telnyx/openclaw-provider` 0.2.0 from npm and wrote a `plugins.installs.telnyx` record. The plugin stayed disabled (not in `plugins.allow`), and we never needed it.
- Confirmed (observed): it copied Voice Call's realtime block into `talk.realtime` (the documented Talk realtime inheritance migration).
- Confirmed (observed): the Gateway logged "Plugin replacement applied" while another scheduled job was starting.
- Confirmed (observed): `TELNYX_API_KEY` was in `~/.openclaw/.env`, unchanged since six days before the run. OpenClaw loads that file for every command, and `docs/help/environment.md` recommends it for provider API keys. The code path below needs that variable.

### Minimal repro (confirmed on main with stub npm and ClawHub registries)

`~/.openclaw/openclaw.json` (the Voice Call entry is optional; the env var alone triggers it):

```json
{ "gateway": { "mode": "local" },
  "plugins": { "entries": { "voice-call": { "enabled": true, "config": { "provider": "telnyx" } } } } }
```

Then run:

```
TELNYX_API_KEY=… openclaw doctor --non-interactive </dev/null
```

- **Expected:** no downloads. Doctor reports that `--fix` would install the plugin.
- **Actual:** doctor runs `npm view @telnyx/openclaw-provider@0.2.0 …`. If npm fails, it falls back to ClawHub, then tries to install.

### Cause (confirmed from source and tests; line numbers from main @ 5958c81)

1. **The preflight path skips the repair-flag check.** `doctor-config-flow.ts:68-76` passes `--fix` only as `repairPrefixedConfig`/`doctorOnlyStateMigrations`. `doctor-config-preflight.ts:85,222,343` runs `runDoctorPluginConvergence` whenever state migrations run, which is every doctor run. That leads to `runPostCorePluginConvergence` → `repairMissingConfiguredPluginInstalls` → `installCandidate`. The other callers of this repair are gated on `shouldRepair` (`doctor-config-flow.ts:497`, `doctor-health-contributions-initial.ts:292`).
2. **An env var alone selects the plugin.** `configured-provider-plugin-ids.ts:27` adds every official provider whose catalog `envVars` is set. The catalog maps `TELNYX_API_KEY` to plugin `telnyx` / `@telnyx/openclaw-provider@0.2.0`.
3. **The nested Voice Call setting is not the trigger.** `plugins.entries.voice-call.config.provider` is never read as a provider reference.

### Why this looks wrong (confirmed)

- `docs/gateway/doctor/running.md` says `--non-interactive` applies "only safe migrations (config normalization + on-disk state moves)".
- The CLI help says "safe migrations only".
- Doctor's own health model classifies this as a `package` repair (`would-install-configured-plugin`, not dry-run safe) whose fix hint is `openclaw doctor --fix`.
- The new install record also feeds the Gateway's next reload pass as a `plugins.installs.*` change, which maps to `reloadPlugins`.

### Proposed fix (patch below)

Unattended runs without repair flags take the existing verify-only branch, the one updates already use. They print:

> Configured plugin telnyx is not installed. Run `openclaw doctor --fix` to install @telnyx/openclaw-provider@0.2.0.

`--fix`/`--repair`/`--yes`, interactive runs that accepted maintenance, and update deferral are unchanged.

The new test calls `doctorCommand` with the npm and ClawHub installers stubbed. It fails on main, 2026.10.5 and 2026.10.8, and passes with the patch. Related doctor suites pass, as do `tsgo:core`, `tsgo:test:src`, oxlint and oxfmt. The new test drives the whole doctor command and takes about 65 s, well over the 5 s target; keeping only its first case would be enough if that's too slow.

### Open questions

- Should interactive runs that accepted maintenance also need `--fix` before downloading?
- Should an API-key env var alone ever select an official provider plugin for installation, especially when another plugin uses the same variable? #142640 made that change for search providers.
- Minor, seen while testing:
  - A failed install with `--fix` is retried three times in one run.
  - The deferral warning for env-selected plugins suggests `openclaw update repair`, which doesn't apply here.

