Related: #184442
Progress-only follow-up to #184452; retry recovery already shipped in #189818.

## What Problem This Solves

During Git-based updates, users cannot see download percentage, received bytes, or speed while a fetch is running.

## User Impact

User impact: source-update users get Git's numeric transfer feedback in the active terminal step, or at most one transfer update every five seconds when output is redirected. `--json` remains a single JSON result without transfer text.

The installed updater must contain this feature before it can display progress. Retry policy and persistent state are unchanged. The maintainer product decision requested in the first ClawSweeper review remains for maintainers before merge.

## Why This Change Was Made

Forward bounded, numeric Git stderr progress through the existing command runner and presenter. Display failures warn once and let the download continue. The existing owners retain retries, cancellation, deadlines, admission and durable history.

The review repair keeps the actual Git error first in saved failure summaries while retaining the complete stderr for existing cause extraction. Ref-lock failures therefore remain actionable, including when SSH authentication details precede Git's fatal summary. Raw stderr capture is unchanged.

## Evidence

Current head: `8c807857867f975d78beb4bc2a762d1cd2405fd5`, including main through `f04a811918d2242de3c033b1bb449bf26ece2495`.

Full build, actual CLI captures and installed upgrade proof below identify parent `0ad0ce34` explicitly. The final follow-up adds five runtime lines preserving SSH failure order and five regression lines. Successful updates never invoke this failure selector; the progress parser, presenter, package/lifecycle and preservation paths are unchanged. Those applicable proofs are reused; the final SSH failure path has separate red/green, native Git and producer/report proof.

- **Actual CLI:** TTY and redirected ref-conflict cases and the successful JSON already-current case passed through `pnpm openclaw` at `0ad0ce34`, using native Git HTTP at 128 KiB/s. History/report causes and unchanged installed refs were checked. Cancellation was also observed (exit 130, no surviving children); its exploratory 15-second settlement assertion failed at 19.928 seconds, as disclosed below.
- **Installed upgrade preservation:** Published latest stable `2026.10.9` → canonical `0ad0ce34` tarball passed the native SQLite-volume survivor: clean host exit 0, all 47 phases, configuration/session/cron preservation, Doctor idempotence and Gateway conversation reads before and after manual restart. All 19,562 immutable package payload files match the installed bytes. The separately pinned archive-oracle correction and bounded fixture are disclosed below.
- **Focused regressions:** 124 tests passed across six suites after the main merge at `0ad0ce34`. The final SSH follow-up passed 68 command/report tests, including the new host-key case that fails on `0ad0ce34`. Existing ref-lock, rejected-tag and public-key-denial regressions remain green. Native Git against an isolated sshd reproduced host-key details before the fatal summary; replay of that captured stderr through the final producer retains the explanation in failure facts, saved detail and reports. The initial progress regressions fail on the original base.
- **Static checks passed:** production, infra-test and CLI-update types; targeted type-aware lint; formatting; whitespace; staged test-line and changed-file line-cap ratchets against `f04a8119` for the complete feature and `0ad0ce34` for the final follow-up.
- **Fresh local Codex reviews:** P0–P2 scoped-clean on the complete eight-file feature after the merge resolution, and on the final SSH follow-up with the producer/redactor/history context supplied. Hosted Codex: the three reported findings have fixes and resolved discussion threads; the latest published hosted receipt still identifies `0ad0ce34`, rather than asserting a new-head clean receipt. [Full CI on parent `0ad0ce34`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/41363696899) passed, including every lint stripe and PostgreSQL conformance. [Current-head CI](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/41365838619) **passed**, and [openclaw/ci-gate](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/41367766407) is **SUCCESS**. The earlier fatal lint errors are fixed.

[ClawSweeper re-review of `8c807857`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/190304#issuecomment-6558379808) is complete: **no actionable code findings; proof sufficient** (proof 5/6, patch quality 4/6). Its only remaining merge prerequisite is the maintainer product decision described above.

Before — published stable CLI, active fetch:

![Published stable CLI shows only the fetch step and elapsed time](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1dd5b1ff-3e70-4b56-b5a3-bbbb53c7032b)

After — actual candidate source CLI under the same native Git transfer-rate limit:

![Candidate CLI shows receiving percentage, bytes, and speed before completion](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/17fe91b5-1afd-4ea9-b8d0-392fab17cbd2)

Failure outcome — deliberate remote tag namespace conflict:

![Saved update outcome retains the actionable cannot-lock-ref diagnosis](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3eb8e1ce-0899-4978-a8f3-9ad9e47c3b8f)

<details>
<summary>Reproduction, package identity, and upgrade proof limits</summary>

The published npm `2026.10.9` CLI is the before capture. Candidate captures invoke the documented source entry directly, without replacing the runner/presenter or injecting output:

```sh
pnpm --silent openclaw update --no-restart --yes --timeout 120
pnpm --silent openclaw update --no-restart --yes --timeout 120 --json
pnpm --silent openclaw update status --json --timeout 5
```

The synthetic native Git HTTP remote serves 160 random 12-KiB objects at 128 KiB/s. The TTY and redirected cases deliberately conflict remote `release/child` with local `release`: CLI exit 1 is expected, wrapper assertions pass, and saved history plus Markdown retain `cannot lock ref`. The live TTY image is replayed from the immutable raw PTY at byte offset 26,993, showing the active spinner at 99%, 1.82 MiB and 124 KiB/s before the outcome. It is distinct from the failure renderer's later stderr replay.

Redirected live updates arrived at 12.190, 17.342 and 22.437 seconds, with 5.152- and 5.095-second gaps, before CLI settlement at 43.62 seconds. The positive JSON case exits 0 (`already-current`) in 33.12 seconds, stdout parses as one JSON object and has no transfer text. Each case performs one native pack download, checks the actual status CLI, and leaves installed refs unchanged. These source CLI fetch cases complement the separate installed-package upgrade below.

An additional real cancellation observation sends SIGINT only to the task-owned actual `openclaw` process during its active 10% Receiving frame. CLI exit is 130, saved reason is `interrupted`, installed refs are unchanged and no original child survives. The native HTTP stream closes after 204,800 of 1,981,538 bytes. Overall settlement takes 19.928 seconds, including post-cancel reporting/triage, so an exploratory `<15 s` assertion fails. No documented 15-second CLI guarantee was found; the failed provisional assertion is retained and no all-green cancellation or latency claim is made.

An initial final-head CLI attempt correctly caught an earlier genuine Git missing-object error in the partial-clone proof checkout. It is retained as a failed exploratory attempt and is not counted. The final CLI fixture is a conventional depth-1 clone with the same `0ad0ce34` HEAD, exact source tree `ce21effbb150e6cdce6d9b9d3abb629564f60b5b`, unchanged build bytes and clean source. Native all-ref connectivity checks pass for both the checkout and synthetic remote before each run; the saved-ref-lock assertions are unchanged.

The final candidate was built with canonical `pnpm build` (exit 0, 446.45 s), then packed with `node scripts/package-openclaw-for-docker.mjs --skip-build --output-dir <proof-dir> --output-name openclaw-pr190304-0ad0ce34.tgz` (exit 0, 159.22 s), including tar safety, npm inventory and runtime import-graph checks.

```text
