## Real behavior proof

- **Behavior or issue addressed:** #185225. A webchat author who does not own an existing shared session is recorded when their `chat.send` is accepted, so that person's Involving me list includes the session.
- **Real environment tested:** macOS 27.0.1, Node v26.12.1, isolated local Gateway from this worktree, two Control UI webchat clients, trusted-proxy identity headers, mock model provider. Not the everyday Gateway.
- **Exact steps or command run after this patch:** Ada (`ada.owner@example.test`) calls `sessions.create` with `visibility: "shared"`. Bee (`bee.guest@example.test`) calls `sessions.list` with `involvingMe: true`, then `chat.send` on Ada's session, then `sessions.list` again, then `sessions.describe` on that session.
- **Evidence after fix:** the Gateway JSON block under Evidence. `senderIsOwner` is false. The participant identity matches Bee's profile id.
- **Observed result after fix:** Bee's profile is not Ada's. Involving me was false before Bee's send and true after `chat.send` returned `started`. Bee's describe readback lists her profile on the shared session.
- **What was not tested:** the everyday operator Gateway, a Gateway restart takeover, driving the ACP bind through a Gateway RPC, and a measurement that `chat.send` returned before reply init. A model request count of 0 does not show that. Recovery adoption of current-boot runs stays out of scope.

## Summary

Record the webchat author at durable accept time, using the same participant helper reply init already uses.

## What Problem This Solves

An ordinary Slack DM could interrupt a heartbeat that was using a tool in the same OpenClaw session. If the tool had already acted, Codex could preserve the interrupted task and the user would get a recovery notice in place of an answer.

## User Impact

Send the DM once. The heartbeat finishes its tool call, then the DM gets its own visible answer with the shared conversation history. No reset, second message, or later heartbeat is needed. Authorized reset and stop commands retain their interrupt behavior.

## Why This Change Was Made

This ports [upstream's wait-on-active admission behavior](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/f9478d593c96c955db06923f4aa6367fb2825b61/src/auto-reply/reply/reply-turn-admission.ts#L475-L506) to the hosted branch's shared heartbeat mode. Admission waits for the owner even across different Slack delivery threads in the same session. If the visible turn has already reserved the reply slot while a heartbeat backend is finishing, it waits for that specific backend to release the transcript. Existing interrupted-task recovery still applies to tasks interrupted for other reasons. When a heartbeat suppresses an interactive chat prompt, the Gateway reports the failed delivery to `ask_user` and declines native secret input before it can claim a later message.

## Evidence

Compared hosted base `1072c072` with the runtime code at `be3e96323` using OpenClaw `2026.10.1-beta.1` with signed Slack Events HTTP input, the bundled Slack and Codex plugins, and local deterministic Slack Web API and Codex app-server peers. Seeded a value in an earlier DM, held a shared heartbeat open after its Slack reaction succeeded, and sent one new DM. The user's DM did not contain the seed.

| Observation | Hosted base | This change |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Native interrupt / steer | 1 / 0 | 0 / 0 |
| Heartbeat tool invocations / observed Slack reactions | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 |
| Result for the first DM | One preservation notice, zero user turns or answers | One visible model reply containing the earlier seed |
| Time from heartbeat completion | — | 1,166 ms to DM turn start, 1,732 ms to Slack post |

The candidate kept the persisted OpenClaw session and lifecycle revision. When its tool catalog changed, the Gateway carried the prior context into the new native Codex thread. The seed was present in the DM's actual `turn/start.input`. This replay used no live Slack workspace, model service, or production tenant.

At `0e18a5a6`, all 335 tests in the six affected suites passed, as did the core TypeScript, changed-file lint, and formatting checks. The routed-DM regression failed on the hosted base and on a partial fix that still allowed a different Slack delivery thread to bypass the heartbeat. The suites also exercise waiter cancellation, backend replacement, and explicit reset.

The same Gateway replay on the previous PR head also reproduced a pending question with no visible Slack prompt. On `be3e96323`, the bundled `ask_user` tool received an immediate delivery failure and an MCP form with a secret field received `cancel`. For each, one signed DM waited while the heartbeat was active, started once after it completed, and received one Slack reply containing the earlier seed. The traces recorded one question invocation and zero native interrupts, steers, or external cancellations. The two new regressions fail against `0e18a5a6`. All 41 tests in their two suites passed on the fix.

